{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update23.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update23.json → valid=true). New direction on completion handling, recorded as a genuine client-facing gap with its harm ceiling stated. The library has machinery for answering accepted requests: the future waiter can select on an optional shutdown channel and return the shutdown error (future.go:103-119), and the leader's step-down cleanup resolves everything already inflight or registered (raft.go:512-520). The gap is that neither covers a request accepted into a channel but not yet consumed: the main loop's shutdown branch clears the leader and returns without draining (raft.go:138-146), and the user-facing entry points never arm the futures' shutdown channel — the only assignments in the captured code are the internal restore futures and the configuration future used by the snapshot routine. Two channels can hold such a request: the verification channel (capacity 64) and the apply channel when the documented buffering option is enabled (capacity equal to the maximum append entries) (api.go:540-544, 566-570; config.go:169-174). So a caller that applies commands with that option enabled, or verifies leadership while the loop is busy, and then shuts the instance down, can be left with a future whose Error() never returns and which no captured code will ever respond to. The unbuffered channels (configuration changes, user snapshot, user restore, bootstrap) cannot accept a request the loop has not received, so they are answered even during shutdown handling — that asymmetry is what isolates the gap. Counterevidence kept explicit: shutdown is documented as not graceful (though it does not say an accepted request may never be answered), the verification channel's entries are normally consumed at once so the case is a race rather than a steady state, dispatched requests remain covered by the leader cleanup, and a caller can mitigate by awaiting Shutdown's future first but cannot drain the library's channels itself. Recorded as one Fact, an active question with a discriminator (enqueue past the loop's capacity with buffering enabled, shut down, observe a future that never returns), and an 'explained' interpretation with the severity bounded to a client-side wait — no claim about entries being lost or reordered.\"}"}}
